WEBVTT

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.050
Imagine Monday,
October seventeenth, twenty eleven.

00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:05.990
It is seven in the morning.

00:00:06.580 --> 00:00:10.150
We want the total rentals during
the hour starting at eight.

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:15.930
The target interval runs from eight,
inclusive, to nine, exclusive.

00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:20.610
Lead time measures the gap from
seven to the interval start.

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:24.070
Its duration measures the
separate hour we aggregate.

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:28.570
The complete count can only be
checked after that interval ends.

00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:33.090
This is an illustrative scenario,
rather than a measured bike record.

00:00:35.192 --> 00:00:39.442
At seven, the requested date
and hour are already known.

00:00:39.912 --> 00:00:43.582
We assume this example is
a nonholiday working day.

00:00:44.192 --> 00:00:47.022
The later rental count is still unknown.

00:00:47.612 --> 00:00:51.052
Casual and registered rentals
cannot become inputs either,

00:00:51.052 --> 00:00:53.342
because they reveal its total.

00:00:53.912 --> 00:00:58.262
Weather measured during the target hour
also arrives later than the request.

00:00:58.812 --> 00:01:02.662
A historical file can place
these values on one row.

00:01:03.152 --> 00:01:07.482
That physical arrangement does not erase
their different availability times.

00:01:08.132 --> 00:01:13.072
The forecast function therefore receives
a calendar description without the later

00:01:13.072 --> 00:01:13.782
answer.

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:20.920
The first operation uses the features
and the current parameters to produce a

00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:21.490
forecast.

00:01:22.060 --> 00:01:26.490
Keep that forecast separate from the
target that arrives after the hour.

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:28.790
Once the target is available,

00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:32.350
we can calculate an error
and a parameter update.

00:01:33.020 --> 00:01:37.290
Recomputing the same input after an
update helps check the arithmetic.

00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:43.050
It does not turn the revised number into
the forecast originally issued at seven.

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:48.740
This order remains necessary when we
replay an old file whose outcomes are

00:01:48.740 --> 00:01:50.150
already visible to us.

00:01:52.333 --> 00:01:54.863
Use one for the working day flag.

00:01:55.353 --> 00:02:00.283
The requested clock hour is eight,
and we choose a fixed divisor of four.

00:02:00.733 --> 00:02:03.023
Eight divided by four gives two,

00:02:03.453 --> 00:02:06.003
so the ordered vector is one, two.

00:02:06.413 --> 00:02:09.943
This scale makes the first
calculation easy to inspect.

00:02:10.393 --> 00:02:14.683
It is a demonstration choice,
not a transform fitted from future data.

00:02:14.933 --> 00:02:18.783
Notice that the feature construction
contains no rental count.

00:02:19.273 --> 00:02:23.333
Later lessons replace this tiny
representation with the project’s forty

00:02:23.333 --> 00:02:24.823
five calendar columns.

00:02:27.333 --> 00:02:31.743
The vector has two positions,
and each position has a declared meaning.

00:02:32.213 --> 00:02:35.263
Its first weight will multiply
the working day value.

00:02:35.433 --> 00:02:38.103
Its second weight will
multiply the scaled hour.

00:02:38.933 --> 00:02:43.053
Swapping those inputs without swapping
their matching parameters changes the

00:02:43.053 --> 00:02:43.543
rule.

00:02:43.793 --> 00:02:45.963
It is not an equivalent serialization.

00:02:46.273 --> 00:02:50.303
That is why a saved predictor
needs an ordered feature schema,

00:02:50.353 --> 00:02:52.043
rather than a bag of numbers.

00:02:52.473 --> 00:02:57.233
The small example makes this contract
visible before a longer calendar vector

00:02:57.233 --> 00:02:59.083
can hide an ordering mistake.

00:03:01.415 --> 00:03:05.305
Now change the request to
the hour from nine until ten.

00:03:05.655 --> 00:03:07.585
Keep the same one hour lead.

00:03:08.155 --> 00:03:12.665
The new issue time is eight,
and the requested clock hour becomes nine.

00:03:13.235 --> 00:03:16.365
Nine divided by four
is two point two five,

00:03:16.695 --> 00:03:19.345
while the working day flag remains one.

00:03:19.835 --> 00:03:23.025
The new interval has
its own later outcome.

00:03:23.335 --> 00:03:26.845
We cannot reuse the previous
interval’s target as its answer.

00:03:27.295 --> 00:03:31.925
We now have a target-free description
of a specific future hour.

00:03:32.415 --> 00:03:37.045
The next explanation attaches weights
and a bias to calculate its estimate.

